<Docs title="FAQ" description="Frequently asked questions about RUDRA." slug="faq">
  <p class="lead">Honest answers to common questions.</p>

  <h2>What is RUDRA?</h2>
  <p>A compiled, statically typed systems language with a native backend. The compiler turns a source file into a native executable.</p>

  <h2>Which platforms are supported?</h2>
  <p>The current 1.1.0 toolchain targets Linux x86_64. macOS (Apple Silicon) and Windows installers are available from the 1.0.1 release. See <a href="/downloads">Downloads</a>.</p>

  <h2>Are the distributed binaries dependency-free?</h2>
  <p>No. The distributed toolchain binaries are dynamically linked executables. The project separately maintains a self-hosting research compiler that is not part of this public distribution; this site makes claims only about the distributed release.</p>

  <h2>Does RUDRA support threads, channels and HTTP?</h2>
  <p>OS threads via <code>spawn</code> are verified. Channels (<code>chan_new</code>, <code>chan_send</code>, <code>chan_recv</code>) and a mutex are present and partially verified. <code>http_get</code> opens real TCP connections but requires a numeric IP (no DNS) and does not do TLS. See <a href="/transparency">Transparency</a> for exact statuses.</p>

  <h2>Is there a package registry?</h2>
  <p>The <code>ruxpkg</code> tool uses a local registry. No public package directory is published on this site, and no third-party packages are listed unless real and verified.</p>

  <h2>What is the license?</h2>
  <p>The RUDRA License: binaries are free to use, including commercially, while the compiler source is proprietary. The programs you build are yours. See the repository LICENSE file.</p>

  <h2>How do I report a bug or request a feature?</h2>
  <p>Use the GitHub Issues tracker for bugs and GitHub Discussions for proposals. See <a href="/developers">Developer Hub</a> and <a href="/community">Community</a>.</p>
</Docs>
